<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">6 May in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 6 May, from 1527 to 1994.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1527 CE <span class="tih-ago">499 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Imperial troops sack Rome</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Unpaid troops of Emperor Charles V stormed Rome, killing thousands and looting churches while Pope Clement VII sheltered in Castel Sant'Angelo. The sack scattered Rome's artists and humbled the papacy.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1840 CE <span class="tih-ago">186 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Penny Black becomes valid for postage</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Britain's prepaid adhesive stamps could now be used on letters. Paying a flat penny in advance, rather than charging recipients by distance, made post cheap and predictable.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1882 CE <span class="tih-ago">144 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Chester Arthur signs the Chinese Exclusion Act</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The law suspended the immigration of Chinese labourers and barred Chinese immigrants from citizenship. It was the first major US law to exclude a group by nationality and was repealed only in 1943.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/paris" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1889 CE <span class="tih-ago">137 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Paris Exposition Universelle opens beneath the Eiffel Tower</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The world's fair marking the centenary of the French Revolution opened in Paris. Its entrance arch, Gustave Eiffel's iron tower, was then the tallest structure in the world.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1910 CE <span class="tih-ago">116 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Edward VII dies and George V becomes king</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Edward VII died at Buckingham Palace after a nine-year reign. Known for his diplomacy and support for the Entente Cordiale, he gave his name to the Edwardian era.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1937 CE <span class="tih-ago">89 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Hindenburg airship bursts into flames at Lakehurst</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The German airship Hindenburg caught fire while mooring in New Jersey, killing 36 people. Newsreel film and radio coverage of the disaster ended public faith in passenger airships.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1954 CE <span class="tih-ago">72 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Roger Bannister runs the first sub-four-minute mile</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">On a windy evening at Oxford's Iffley Road track, the medical student Roger Bannister ran the mile in 3 minutes 59.4 seconds, helped by pacemakers Chris Brasher and Chris Chataway.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1994 CE <span class="tih-ago">32 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Channel Tunnel is formally opened</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Queen Elizabeth II and President François Mitterrand opened the 50 km rail tunnel under the Channel. It gave Britain its first fixed link to the Continent since the Ice Age.</p></div></li></ol>
